\begin{center}
{
\Large
Sorting Quiz Practice Problems
}
\end{center}

\begin{note}
In this notes packet, we will study \emph{comparison based} sorting algorithms;
that is, sorting algorithms that use the comparison operators \lstinline{<}, \lstinline{<=}, \lstinline{>}, \lstinline{>=} to perform the sort.
A standard result (which you will not have to prove),
is that any comparison based sorting algorithm requires at least $\Omega(n\log n)$ comparison operations.
We will see that both merge sort and quick sort are ``optimal'' in the sense that merge sort has worst case runtime $O(n\log n)$ and quicksort has average case runtime $O(n\log n)$,
both of which match the lower bound.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
The primary use case of sorting is to be able to run binary search later.
Recall that binary search takes time $O(\log n)$,
but requires sorted data.
In order to get this very fast runtime,
we must pay a $\Omega(n\log n)$ ``setup cost'' penalty to sort the data.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
You should never implement a sorting algorithm in the real world.
The built-in python \lstinline{.sort()} and \lstinline{sorted()} are much faster than anything you will write for two reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
\item They are highly optimized and written in C instead of Python to reduce the constant factor.
    (Most speed-sensitive functions in pytohn are written in C and python is only a ``wrapper'' around the C code.)
\item Python uses TimSort.
    TimSort uses special tricks to reduce the runtime from $O(n\log n)$ to $O(n)$ in certain common situations (like if the data is partially sorted).
    TimSort was invented by Tim Peters,
    who is widely considered the second most important person in the python world after Guido.
\end{enumerate}
We are studying sorting algorithms not so that you can write them,
but to get you more practice using recursion on real world applications.
Also, sorting algorithms form the foundation for more complex algorithms you will learn later.
\end{note}

\clearpage
\section{Merge Sort}
\begin{note}
\emph{Merge sort} splits the input into two halves, recursively
sorts each half, and merges the two sorted halves into a sorted
list. This gives the recurrence
\[
T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + O(n).
\]
By case 2 of the master theorem, $T(n) = O(n \log n)$.
\end{note}
